course: dictionary
lesson: 14747
title: Prone: two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. Prone is one of them. «The village is prone to flooding in winter.» Here prone means likely to have something, often something bad.
explain: The same word can describe how a body is lying. «The swimmer lay prone on the warm sand, face down.» Lying prone means lying flat on your front. Look at the words around prone to decide: is it about a risk, or about how someone is lying?
explain: Prone is often joined to another word, such as accident-prone. «Sam is accident-prone, so he always wears his helmet.» It means Sam often has accidents. Prone to something means likely to have it.
fact: Prone means likely to have something, or likely to do something, often something bad.
fact: «Prone to colds» means you often catch colds.
fact: Lying prone means lying flat on your front, face down.
fact: The opposite of prone, when lying down, is supine: lying flat on your back, face up.
fact: Prone is often used with «to»: prone to, accident-prone, injury-prone.
exercise: «The island is prone to earthquakes.» What does prone mean here? | Safe from earthquakes forever | Lying face down on the ground | Painted in bright colours | *Likely to have earthquakes
exercise: «The swimmer lay prone on the sand.» What does prone mean here? | Standing very tall | *Lying flat on her front, face down | Asleep under a tree | Lying face up
exercise: «Ella is injury-prone, so she must be careful in football.» What does injury-prone mean? | Is the best player on the team | *Often gets hurt | Likes to play injuries | Never gets hurt
test: «The old bridge is prone to cracks after storms.» What does prone to cracks mean? | *Likely to get cracks | Painted in bright colours | Very new and clean | Strong enough to never break
test: «Mia was told to lie prone for the first aid lesson.» What does prone mean here? | Lying on her back | Sitting up straight | *Lying face down | Jumping up and down
exam: «The field is flood-prone, so the path is often under water.» What does flood-prone mean? | Very dry all year | Covered in flowers | *Likely to flood | Lying face down in the grass
